message.content:
Now I understand the vulnerability better. The fix in commit 36118b6 drops the unnecessary utf8_replacement_char that was added when input runs out in the middle of a unicode escape. The key change is in the restructured code: when we're reading hex digits and run out of input, we just `goto out` without adding any replacement char.

But the question is: does this cause a crash/memory safety issue? Let me look at the code more carefully to see if the unnecessary addition of the replacement char could cause a buffer overflow or other memory safety issue.

Looking at the code at lines 796-803:
```c
if (!ADVANCE_CHAR(str, tok) || !PEEK_CHAR(c, tok))
{
    /* Clean up any pending chars */
    if (tok->got_hi_surrogate &&
        strcmp(tok->pb->buf, (char *)utf8_replacement_char))
            printbuf_memappend_fast(
                tok->pb, (char *)utf8_replacement_char, 3);
    goto out;
}
```

The `printbuf_memappend_fast` function appends to the printbuf. If the printbuf doesn't have enough space, it could potentially overflow. But `printbuf_memappend_fast` likely resizes the buffer. Let me check:

